# The project video, as a cue sheet.
#
# Every frame is the real plugin rendering through the real FFGL sequence -- this
# is the rig being *operated*, not a mock-up. Render with:
#
#   ./build/esctest --sequence /tmp/esc-frames --script tools/video.cues \
#                   --size 1920x1080 --seconds 62 --fps 30
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Every value here is the 0..1 the host sees, not the physical quantity -- see
# Controls.h for why there is no other kind. The physical value each one lands on
# is in the comment where it matters.
#
# ## The argument of the piece
#
# The plugin's whole claim is that the fractals are what a feedback loop settles
# into rather than anything it draws, so the video has to make that visible
# rather than assert it. Hence the order:
#
#   1. a rig switched on, with the operator's hands OFF -- it builds, and then
#      it STOPS, which is the theorem the plugin is built on;
#   2. the hands arrive, and it never settles again;
#   3. a gasket assembling itself out of sensor grain, which is the moment that
#      proves nothing is drawing it;
#   4. the same machine reconfigured -- snowflake, seeded, Julia, a deep zoom,
#      a globe -- and last the reaction that keeps reorganising for ever.
#
# The rig is never reset between sections. The frame store carries its history
# across every change, so each one DISSOLVES as the loop re-converges from what
# was already going round it. That is what the rig does, and it is worth seeing.

# ------------------------------------------------------- 0s: switch it on
# A camera pointed at a screen, and a grid to give the lens an edge to find.
# Zoom below 1:1 on purpose -- a tunnel is made of the frame's own edge, so the
# camera has to be able to see past it.
0.0   Rig=0
0.0   Symmetry=0.0
0.0   Mirror Wedge=0.0
0.0   Zoom=0.208          # 0.98 per field
0.0   Rotate=0.699        # 0.01 rad per field
0.0   Pan X=0.5
0.0   Pan Y=0.5
0.0   Speed=0.5
0.0   Focus=0.085         # 0.43% of the short edge
0.0   Lens=0.5
0.0   Vignette=0.08
0.0   Gain=0.600          # 0.96
0.0   Pedestal=0.5
0.0   Gamma=0.5
0.0   Hue Rotate=0.658    # 0.002 turns per field
0.0   Saturation=0.55
0.0   Clip=0.786
0.0   Noise=0.0
0.0   Decay=0.050
0.0   Inject=3            # grid
0.0   Inject Level=0.550
0.0   Inject Size=0.720
0.0   Inject X=0.5
0.0   Inject Y=0.5
0.0   Palette=0           # the signal's own colour
0.0   Sphere=0.0
0.0   Opacity=1.0

# Hands OFF. The rig converges on its attractor and then stands still, which is
# Banach rather than a bug -- and showing it is the only honest way to explain
# what Drift is for.
0.0   Drift=0.0
0.0   Drift Rate=0.520

# ------------------------------------------------------- 7s: the hands
# The operator arrives. Nothing else changes; the picture stops being a still.
7.0..11.0   Drift=0.0..0.55

# A breath through 1:1, so the tunnel turns itself inside out and back.
11.0..15.0  Zoom=0.208..0.760
15.0..18.0  Zoom=0.760..0.240

# ------------------------------------------- 18s: a gasket, out of grain
# The moment the piece exists for. Three half-scale taps on a triangle, fed
# nothing but sensor noise -- and the Sierpinski gasket assembles itself.
# Nothing here draws a gasket.
18.0  Rig=1
18.0  Inject=4            # noise: the loop's only food
18.0  Inject Level=0.260  # 0.040 -- grain is the SEED, not the fuel
18.0  Inject Size=1.0
18.0  Zoom=0.5            # exactly 1.0 -- the taps supply all the contraction
18.0  Rotate=0.5          # exactly 0
18.0  Gain=0.700          # 1.12, over unity so the attractor fills
18.0  Decay=0.0
18.0  Noise=0.10
18.0  Focus=0.088
18.0  Vignette=0.10
18.0  Drift=0.50

# ------------------------------------------------ 26s: the snowflake
# Four third-scale taps make the Koch CURVE. The three-fold fold -- a mirror
# wedge in front of the lens -- puts it on all three sides of a triangle.
26.0  Rig=2
26.0  Symmetry=0.18       # 3-fold
26.0  Gain=0.812          # 1.30: a curve is measure zero and needs more than an area
26.0  Decay=0.153
26.0  Inject Level=0.140  # a curve needs less food than an area
26.0  Drift=0.50
26.0  Hue Rotate=0.600

# ------------------------------------------------ 33s: a rig from a number
# Seeded contractions. Nudging the seed walks through whole different rigs, and
# every one is framed, because a seeded attractor's bounds are measured.
33.0  Rig=6
33.0  Symmetry=0.0
33.0  Taps=0.30           # 4 taps
33.0  Gain=0.594
33.0  Inject Level=0.140  # quieter grain: louder and it competes with the attractor
33.0  Vignette=0.20
33.0  Palette=4           # spectrum
33.0  Drift=0.55
33.0..39.0  Seed=0.402..0.740

# ------------------------------------------------------ 39s: Julia
# The iterator bank: analogue multipliers wired as a complex square. Drift walks
# the constant, so it is a different Julia set every field and never settles.
39.0  Rig=7
39.0  Gain=0.0            # nothing goes round the geometric path
39.0  Decay=0.55
39.0  Inject=5            # the bank's own picture
39.0  Inject Level=0.869
39.0  Inject Size=1.0
39.0  Palette=3           # ember
39.0  Iterations=0.571
39.0  Escape Zoom=0.0
39.0  Drift=0.60
39.0  Noise=0.0

# --------------------------------------------- 44s: the deep zoom
# The one place in the plugin that holds a coordinate, and so the only place
# with a precision limit. Extended is double-float arithmetic; this is a real
# 1e6 magnification, not a crop.
44.0  Rig=8
44.0  Palette=2           # ice
44.0  Iterations=0.850    # ~1060: a starved count looks exactly like a precision failure
44.0  Precision=1         # extended
44.0  Decay=0.20
44.0  Drift=0.30
44.0..50.0  Escape Zoom=0.0..0.462   # 1 -> 1e6

# ------------------------------------------------------ 50s: the globe
# The rescan: the rig's screen is a sphere and the camera is looking at it. The
# loop underneath is an ordinary mirror rig, still running, so the texture on
# the ball is alive rather than a still being spun.
50.0  Rig=9
50.0  Zoom=0.208
50.0  Rotate=0.699
50.0  Gain=0.600
50.0  Decay=0.050
50.0  Inject=3
50.0  Inject Level=0.550
50.0  Inject Size=0.720
50.0  Palette=1           # phosphor: a white grid hue-rotates to nothing, grey is a fixed point
50.0  Sphere=1.0
50.0  Tilt=0.611
50.0  Spin=0.612
50.0  Light=0.5
50.0  Precision=0
50.0  Escape Zoom=0.0
50.0  Drift=0.40

# --------------------------------------- 56s: the reaction, and out
# Chroma gain above unity is a reaction term, the defocus is diffusion, the clip
# is the ceiling. A reaction-diffusion system whose domains are colours, and it
# keeps reorganising for as long as you leave it.
56.0  Rig=0
56.0  Sphere=0.0
56.0  Zoom=0.300
56.0  Focus=0.148
56.0  Gain=0.660          # 1.056 -- the band is narrow
56.0  Saturation=0.700    # 1.40 -- ABOVE unity, and this is the whole preset
56.0  Clip=0.500
56.0  Decay=0.0
56.0  Noise=0.25
56.0  Inject=0            # nothing: the loop finds its own picture
56.0  Inject Level=0.0
56.0  Hue Rotate=0.700
56.0  Drift=0.20
